## Summary

This is a **Maven** project (Spring PetClinic REST converted to OfficeFloor). There is no Gradle build. The wrapper `./mvnw` is present (`pom.xml` at repo root, OfficeFloor 4.0.2, Spring Boot).

The business-day "registration date" rule already exists in production code as a package-private helper class, and it is exercised by JUnit 5 acceptance tests. Importantly: the **production code only rolls off weekends — it does NOT handle public holidays**, while the tests already have a pinned holiday calendar and a `toBusinessDay` helper that skips holidays too. This is a latent gap (see Cp47 note below).

---

### 1. Registration-date -> business-day logic (production)

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/RegistrationDate.java`** — the core rule. Weekends only; no holiday handling.

```java
final class RegistrationDate {

    private RegistrationDate() {
    }

    /** Roll a Saturday or Sunday forward to the next Monday; a weekday is returned unchanged. */
    static LocalDate toBusinessDay(LocalDate date) {
        DayOfWeek day = date.getDayOfWeek();
        if (day == DayOfWeek.SATURDAY) {
            return date.plusDays(2);
        }
        if (day == DayOfWeek.SUNDAY) {
            return date.plusDays(1);
        }
        return date;
    }

    /**
     * The effective, business-day-adjusted registration date for a create request: the supplied
     * date, else the server's current date, rolled forward off any weekend.
     */
    static LocalDate effective(OwnerFieldsDto request) {
        LocalDate supplied = request.getRegistrationDate();
        return toBusinessDay(supplied != null ? supplied : LocalDate.now());
    }
}
```

**Callers of `RegistrationDate.effective(...)`:**

- **`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/BuildOwner.java`** — sets the adjusted date on the new `Owner`:
```java
public void service(@Val OwnerFieldsDto request, OwnerMapper ownerMapper, Out<Owner> built) {
    Owner owner = ownerMapper.toOwner(request);
    owner.setRegistrationDate(RegistrationDate.effective(request));
    built.set(owner);
}
```

- **`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/RequireDailyOwnerLimit.java`** — the daily create-limit counts per adjusted business day:
```java
public void service(@Val OwnerFieldsDto request, OwnerRepository ownerRepository)
        throws OwnerDailyLimitExceededException {
    LocalDate businessDay = RegistrationDate.effective(request);
    long createdThatDay = ownerRepository.findAll().stream()
            .filter(existing -> businessDay.equals(existing.getRegistrationDate()))
            .count();
    if (createdThatDay >= DAILY_LIMIT) {
        throw new OwnerDailyLimitExceededException(businessDay, createdThatDay);
    }
}
```

Related supporting classes (not business-day logic themselves, but consume the registration date): `AssignMembershipNumber.java`, `RequireRegistrationDateNotFuture.java`, `AssignBulkSignupWarning.java` in the same `.../rest/function/owner/` directory, plus escalation types `OwnerRegistrationDateInvalidException.java` / `...ExceptionHandler.java` under `.../rest/escalation/`.

---

### 2. Tests for the business-day rule

All tests live under `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/`. They are JUnit 5, each tagged with a `@Tag("cpNN")`, and extend `AcceptanceBase`.

**`Cp07Tests.java`** (weekend roll to Monday — the direct test of the rule):
```java
@Test
void coreWeekendRollsToMonday() throws Exception {
    ObjectNode o = ownerNode();
    o.put("registrationDate", "2026-01-03"); // Saturday
    int id = createOwnerOk(o);
    getOwner(id).andExpect(jsonPath("$.registrationDate").value("2026-01-05"));
}

@Test
void functionalityWeekdayKept() throws Exception {
    ObjectNode o = ownerNode();
    o.put("registrationDate", "2026-01-06"); // Tuesday
    int id = createOwnerOk(o);
    getOwner(id).andExpect(jsonPath("$.registrationDate").value("2026-01-06"));
}
```

**`Cp20Tests.java`** (default date is a business day — invariant only, never Sat/Sun):
```java
@Test
void coreDefaultDateIsBusinessDay() throws Exception {
    int id = createOwnerOk(ownerNode());
    DayOfWeek dow = LocalDate.parse(fetchOwner(id).get("registrationDate").asText()).getDayOfWeek();
    assertTrue(dow != DayOfWeek.SATURDAY && dow != DayOfWeek.SUNDAY, dow.toString());
```

**`Cp47Tests.java`** (holiday-business-day — asserts the weekend invariant as a proxy; see class Javadoc):
```java
/** holiday-business-day: the default registration date rolls past public holidays as well as
 * weekends. The specific holiday calendar and the server clock can't be driven black-box, so assert
 * the invariant that subsumes it: the default registrationDate is never a Saturday or Sunday. */
@Tag("cp47")
class Cp47Tests extends AcceptanceBase {

	@Test
	void coreDefaultDateIsBusinessDay() throws Exception {
		DayOfWeek dow = LocalDate.parse(
				fetchOwner(createOwnerOk(structuredOwner())).get("registrationDate").asText()).getDayOfWeek();
		assertTrue(dow != DayOfWeek.SATURDAY && dow != DayOfWeek.SUNDAY, dow.toString());
	}
}
```

**`Cp19Tests.java`** (daily-limit counted per adjusted business day) exercises the same helper indirectly via `RequireDailyOwnerLimit`.

**Test helper — `AcceptanceBase.java`** (`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java`) has its own `toBusinessDay` that *does* skip holidays:
```java
/** Roll a date forward over weekends and pinned holidays to the next business day. */
protected LocalDate toBusinessDay(LocalDate d) {
    while (d.getDayOfWeek() == DayOfWeek.SATURDAY || d.getDayOfWeek() == DayOfWeek.SUNDAY
            || HOLIDAYS.contains(d)) {
        d = d.plusDays(1);
    }
    return d;
}
```

---

### 3. Public-holiday references

- **Production code: NONE.** `grep -rniI holiday src/main` returns nothing. `RegistrationDate.toBusinessDay` handles only Saturday/Sunday. So the "probably none yet" expectation is confirmed for main source — a holiday calendar would be net-new work.
- **Test code: YES, a pinned calendar already exists** in `AcceptanceBase.java` (lines 239-242):
```java
/** Fixed public holidays the business-day roll skips (year of the run). */
protected static final Set<LocalDate> HOLIDAYS = Set.of(
        LocalDate.parse("2026-01-01"), LocalDate.parse("2026-01-26"),
        LocalDate.parse("2026-04-25"), LocalDate.parse("2026-12-25"), LocalDate.parse("2026-12-28"));
```
This is Australian-flavoured (matches the pinned `CITY_REGION` NSW/VIC/QLD data in the same file). The test-side `toBusinessDay` and `Cp47Tests` are written around it, but production `RegistrationDate` never consults any such set — so a supplied-date-lands-on-a-holiday case is not yet enforced by production code.

---

### Build tool & running the test suite

- **Build tool:** Maven. Root `pom.xml`; wrapper scripts `mvnw` / `mvnw.cmd`. No surefire/failsafe customization in the pom (default Surefire, so `*Tests.java` are picked up).
- **Run all tests:** `./mvnw test` (or `./mvnw clean install` per readme.md line 218, which runs the full build incl. tests).
- **Run a single business-day test class:** `./mvnw test -Dtest=Cp07Tests` (similarly `Cp20Tests`, `Cp47Tests`, `Cp19Tests`).
- Tests are JUnit 5 tagged (`@Tag("cp07")`, etc.), so they can also be filtered with `-Dgroups=cp07` if desired.
- Run the app (readme lines 25/137): `./mvnw spring-boot:run`.

